<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Corbeil (1258)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1258">1258</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Corbeil was signed in May 1258 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a>. Louis renounced the French crown's claims to suzerainty over the Catalan counties, and James gave up most of his claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and Provence, fixing the border roughly along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 11 May 1258 at Corbeil, today Corbeil-Essonnes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a>. It settled overlapping claims inherited from earlier centuries. The French king claimed, as heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, feudal overlordship over the counties known to historians as the Hispanic March, the part of the March of Gothia that lay within what was called Catalonia from the 12th century onwards. The counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, for their part, held or claimed rights over a wide arc of lordships in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, some going back to enfeoffments of 1112 and 1179 and to the inheritance of Douce I of Provence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Louis IX renounced his claim to overlordship of the counties of Catalonia. James I gave up his claims to Fenouillet-du-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razes">Razès</a> and Peyrepertuse, with the castles of Puilaurens, Fenouillet, Castellfisel, Peyrepertuse and Quéribus, and his feudal overlordship over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, Saint Gilles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quercy">Quercy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, Razès, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a>, Lauragais, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiermes">Termes</a> and Ménerbes, Agde and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a>, and Rouergue, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millau">Millau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gevaudan">Gévaudan</a>. He kept the viscounty of Carlat and the lordship of Montpellier with the barony of Aumelas. The treaty also arranged the marriage of James's daughter Isabella to Philip, son of Louis IX.</p><p>When he ratified the document on 16 July 1258, James refused to give up his feudal rights over the County of Foix, which the text had at first included, arguing that Foix was not under the overlordship of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. On 17 July he also renounced his hereditary rights to the County of Provence, then an imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, in favour of Margaret, daughter of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-provence">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence</a>, and wife of the French king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The direct result was to separate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a> for good from the politics of what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The close cultural and economic ties between the lands that became Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> faded gradually after it. A further effect was that Provence could pass to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a> and, once that house died out, be incorporated into France.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Corbeil (1258)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Treaty of Corbeil</b> of 1258 Louis IX of France renounced his old claims to suzerainty over the Catalan counties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
